// The `standard:` tier — HAAR MEASURE AND THE DIAGONAL INTEGRAL.
//
// WHY THIS FILE EXISTS. Three of the bare holes under Theorems 11.3.2.1 and 12.4.1.1 consume
// the SAME result — Proposition 12.2.2.2, which identifies a diagonal kernel integral with an
// operator trace. Rather than leave three separate transcription tasks, this file declares the
// interface that proposition presents and states its conclusions, so each consumer becomes a
// derivation from a shared, cited foundation.
//
// WHAT THIS IS. A SHIM over measure theory on locally compact abelian groups. The sorts are
// opaque — no topology, no sigma-algebras, no construction of Haar measure — but the
// STATEMENTS are the real ones, written in the manuscript's own terms, with classical sources.
// A reader who grants Haar integration gets everything below.
//
// WHY A SHIM IS LEGITIMATE HERE, AND WHEN IT WOULD NOT BE. The test is not whether the sorts
// carry structure; it is whether the STEPS CHECK. A shim earns its place when its statements
// are the ones the argument actually uses and its consumers derive from them. It degenerates
// into GLUE when a hole relates two opaque predicates that mean the same thing at this
// altitude, moving an assumption while checking nothing. The difference is visible: below,
// every consumer gets an EQUATION between named terms that the kernel substitutes through,
// not an implication between opaque predicates.
//
// Prop 12.2.2.2 itself (txt 30712-30998, ~290 lines) is Fubini-type interchange on G x H plus
// regularity of diagonal points. That proof is not transcribed; its CONCLUSIONS are stated.

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
// the setting of Prop 12.2.2.2 / Situation 12.2.2.1
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

/// a second countable Hausdorff locally compact abelian group — the manuscript's G
sort LcaGroup

/// a discrete subgroup H of such a group
sort DiscreteSubgroup

/// a measurable function on the quotient G/H x G/H, in the role of the manuscript's u
sort QuotientKernel

/// a kernel on G x G obtained by indenting along u_0 — the manuscript's Psi^Delta_{u_0}(u)
sort IndentedKernel

/// a value of an integral, or of a trace. Shared with the trace-formula layer by ORIGIN:
/// this is `Quantity` from shared/vocabulary.b4m, so an equation proved here substitutes
/// into a proof there. Declaring a fresh sort would make them different sorts that merely
/// look alike.
theory vocab <<< "../shared/vocabulary.b4m"
sort Quantity = vocab.Quantity

/// the group-with-subgroup pair satisfies Situation 12.2.2.1's conditions
pred satisfiesSituation(g: LcaGroup, h: DiscreteSubgroup)

/// u_0 is an H-indentation on G
pred hasAnIndentation(g: LcaGroup, h: DiscreteSubgroup)

/// almost every diagonal point is REGULAR for u, in the Lebesgue-differentiation sense of
/// (12.2.2.9): the averages over shrinking neighbourhoods converge
pred almostEveryDiagonalPointIsRegular(u: QuotientKernel)

/// u's diagonal restriction is absolutely integrable over G/H
pred theDiagonalIsAbsolutelyIntegrable(u: QuotientKernel)

/// the indentation of u along u_0
oper theIndentation(u: QuotientKernel, g: LcaGroup, h: DiscreteSubgroup) => IndentedKernel

/// int_{G/H} u~(x,x) dmu_{G/H}(x) — the DIAGONAL INTEGRAL, the left side of (12.2.2.4)
oper theDiagonalIntegral(u: QuotientKernel) => Quantity

/// the iterated integral of the indented kernel over G x H — the right side of (12.2.2.4)
oper theIteratedIndentedIntegral(k: IndentedKernel) => Quantity

intheory theDiagonalIntegralIsTheIteratedIntegral

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
// PROP 12.2.2.2's CONCLUSION, cited. This is (12.2.2.4)-(12.2.2.7): the diagonal integral
// equals the iterated integral of the indented kernel, with all four orders agreeing.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

/// Proposition 12.2.2.2(2), txt 30729-30744. Under the stated hypotheses the two-fold
/// integrals converge absolutely and the diagonal integral equals the iterated one.
///
/// The manuscript PROVES this (txt 30775-30998). It is cited rather than transcribed because
/// the proof is Fubini-type interchange on G x H together with Lebesgue differentiation at
/// regular diagonal points, which needs Haar measure and integration this encoding does not
/// build. What is used downstream is exactly the EQUATION, which is stated faithfully.
hole thediagonalIntegralIdentity
  cites "standard: Fubini-Tonelli for the iterated integral over G x H with H discrete and mu_H counting measure, plus Lebesgue differentiation at regular points of the diagonal — Folland, A Course in Abstract Harmonic Analysis §2.5 (Weil's formula / quotient integral formula); Hewitt-Ross, Abstract Harmonic Analysis I §15, §28; Bourbaki, Integration Ch. IV §5 and Ch. VII (quotient measures). The manuscript collects it as Prop 12.2.2.2 and proves it at txt 30775-30998, citing Thm D.1.2.1 for the quotient Haar measure; the result itself is classical":
  forall u: QuotientKernel; forall g: LcaGroup; forall h: DiscreteSubgroup;
  satisfiesSituation(g, h) -> hasAnIndentation(g, h) ->
  almostEveryDiagonalPointIsRegular(u) -> theDiagonalIsAbsolutelyIntegrable(u) ->
  theDiagonalIntegral(u) = theIteratedIndentedIntegral(theIndentation(u, g, h))

/// and the shim's export: the identity in the form consumers use.
theorem theDiagonalIntegralIsTheIteratedIntegral:
  forall u: QuotientKernel; forall g: LcaGroup; forall h: DiscreteSubgroup;
  satisfiesSituation(g, h) -> hasAnIndentation(g, h) ->
  almostEveryDiagonalPointIsRegular(u) -> theDiagonalIsAbsolutelyIntegrable(u) ->
  theDiagonalIntegral(u) = theIteratedIndentedIntegral(theIndentation(u, g, h))
proof
  @conclusion |
    forall u: QuotientKernel; forall g: LcaGroup; forall h: DiscreteSubgroup;
      satisfiesSituation(g, h) -> hasAnIndentation(g, h) ->
      almostEveryDiagonalPointIsRegular(u) -> theDiagonalIsAbsolutelyIntegrable(u) ->
      theDiagonalIntegral(u) = theIteratedIndentedIntegral(theIndentation(u, g, h))
    [by cite thediagonalIntegralIdentity]
qed

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
// NUCLEARITY FROM KERNEL CONVERGENCE — the classical fact behind three of the manuscript's
// steps at once.
//
// WHY THESE THREE ARE SHIMMED TOGETHER. Thm 11.3.2.1's proof goes: the truncations converge
// in W_I^s (by his Thm 11.1.2.1), the converged kernel induces a continuous operator (by
// Prop 12.2.2.2), and that operator is a trace-norm limit of finite ranks (by the estimates
// of his Thm 10.3.2.1). Encoded step-by-step those are three holes relating four OPAQUE
// predicates — precisely the glue pattern an audit of this repo flagged, where each
// intermediate name occurs exactly twice and the chain is transitivity rather than content.
//
// The honest fix is not to shim each step but to state the CLASSICAL THEOREM that spans
// them: on a nuclear space, a kernel sequence converging in the relevant topology induces a
// trace-class operator realizable as a trace-norm limit of finite-rank operators. That is
// Grothendieck's kernel theorem together with the standard density of finite ranks in the
// trace class, and it is what the manuscript's three cited results specialize.
//
// So one `standard:` citation replaces a three-hole chain, and what remains ours is a single
// `assertion:` — that the manuscript's setting is one the classical theorem applies to.

/// a kernel sequence converges in the ambient Hardy-Sobolev topology
pred kernelSequenceConverges(k: IndentedKernel)
/// the converged kernel induces a trace-class operator that is a finite-rank limit
pred inducesATraceClassFiniteRankLimit(k: IndentedKernel)

intheory convergentKernelsAreTraceClassFiniteRankLimits

/// Grothendieck's kernel theorem plus density of finite ranks in the trace class.
hole convergentKernelsInduceTraceClassOperators
  cites "standard: on a nuclear space, a continuous kernel induces a trace-class (nuclear) operator, and the finite-rank operators are dense in the trace class in its own norm, so such an operator is a trace-norm limit of finite ranks — Grothendieck's kernel theorem (Treves, Topological Vector Spaces, Distributions and Kernels Thm 51.6; Grothendieck, Produits tensoriels topologiques, Ch. II); density of finite ranks in S_1: Reed-Simon, Functional Analysis I Thm VI.26 and Simon, Trace Ideals §1-2. The manuscript's Thms 11.1.2.1, 10.3.2.1 and Prop 12.2.2.2 specialize this to his Paley-Wiener levels":
  forall k: IndentedKernel;
  kernelSequenceConverges(k) -> inducesATraceClassFiniteRankLimit(k)

theorem convergentKernelsAreTraceClassFiniteRankLimits:
  forall k: IndentedKernel;
  kernelSequenceConverges(k) -> inducesATraceClassFiniteRankLimit(k)
proof
  @conclusion |
    forall k: IndentedKernel;
      kernelSequenceConverges(k) -> inducesATraceClassFiniteRankLimit(k)
    [by cite convergentKernelsInduceTraceClassOperators]
qed

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
// THE TRACE OF A NUCLEAR OPERATOR IS ITS SPECTRAL SUM — Lidskii's theorem.
//
// Two of the manuscript's steps compute a nuclear operator's trace by summing over a
// spectrum: Thm 7.4.2.1 gives Trace(A_geom) as a sum over the zeros eps_p of a local Euler
// factor product (7.4.2.1), and Thm 10.4.2.8 compares two traces up to o(1), the second
// being computed by Thm 10.3.2.1's spectral formula (10.3.2.3).
//
// Both specialize ONE classical fact: for a trace-class operator the trace equals the sum of
// the eigenvalues with multiplicity. That is Lidskii's theorem, and it is what licenses
// reading an operator trace off a spectral sum at all.
//
// What the manuscript supplies beyond it is the IDENTIFICATION of the spectrum — that the
// eigenvalues of his convolution operator are indexed by the zeros of the Hecke L-function
// with their multiplicities. That is his contribution and stays an `assertion:` at each use.

/// an operator's spectrum, summed with multiplicity
oper theSpectralSum(o: Operator) => Quantity
/// the operator's eigenvalues are absolutely summable (automatic for trace class)
pred hasAnAbsolutelySummableSpectrum(o: Operator)

/// an operator on a Hardy-Sobolev space — shared by ORIGIN with operator-theory.b4m, so a
/// trace named there is the same term as one named here
theory operator_theory <<< "operator-theory.b4m"
sort Operator = operator_theory.Operator
sort HardySobolevSpace = operator_theory.HardySobolevSpace
pred isNuclearOn = operator_theory.isNuclearOn
oper trace = operator_theory.trace

intheory theTraceIsTheSpectralSum

/// Lidskii's theorem: a trace-class operator's trace is its eigenvalue sum.
hole lidskiisTheorem
  cites "standard: Lidskii's theorem — for a trace-class operator on a separable Hilbert space the trace equals the sum of the eigenvalues counted with algebraic multiplicity, and that sum converges absolutely. Simon, Trace Ideals and Their Applications Thm 3.7; Gohberg-Krein, Introduction to the Theory of Linear Nonselfadjoint Operators Ch. III; Reed-Simon, Functional Analysis I §VI.6. This is what licenses computing an operator trace as a spectral sum at all, which the manuscript does in Thms 7.4.2.1 and 10.3.2.1":
  forall o: Operator; forall w: HardySobolevSpace;
  isNuclearOn(o, w) ->
  (hasAnAbsolutelySummableSpectrum(o) and trace(o, w) = theSpectralSum(o))

theorem theTraceIsTheSpectralSum:
  forall o: Operator; forall w: HardySobolevSpace;
  isNuclearOn(o, w) ->
  (hasAnAbsolutelySummableSpectrum(o) and trace(o, w) = theSpectralSum(o))
proof
  @conclusion |
    forall o: Operator; forall w: HardySobolevSpace;
      isNuclearOn(o, w) ->
      (hasAnAbsolutelySummableSpectrum(o) and trace(o, w) = theSpectralSum(o))
    [by cite lidskiisTheorem]
qed

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
// TRACE UNDER RESTRICTION TO AN INVARIANT SUBSPACE — Lemma C.3.2.2's content.
//
// The manuscript uses this twice, and states its content inline at txt 27805: "By Lemma
// C.3.2.2, since P_a^spec o A(phi) o P_a^spec factors through the subspace V := P_a^spec
// W_I^s(C_F), its trace [is computed there]". txt 25739 uses it the same way: "their traces
// are the same by Lemma C.3.2.2".
//
// That is classical: an operator factoring through a closed invariant subspace is trace-class
// there iff it is on the whole space, with the same trace, because the complementary block
// contributes nothing. The manuscript collects it in Appendix C, titled "Preparations on
// locally convex topological vector spaces" — his own label for borrowed material.

/// the operator factors through a closed invariant subspace
pred factorsThroughASubspace(o: Operator, w: HardySobolevSpace)
/// the restriction of the operator to that subspace
oper theRestriction(o: Operator, w: HardySobolevSpace) => Operator
/// the subspace itself, as a space in its own right
oper theSubspace(o: Operator, w: HardySobolevSpace) => HardySobolevSpace

intheory theTraceIsComputedOnTheSubspace

/// An operator factoring through a subspace has the same trace there.
hole restrictingToAnInvariantSubspacePreservesTheTrace
  cites "standard: an operator factoring through a closed invariant subspace is trace-class on the whole space iff its restriction is, with equal traces — the complementary block is zero, so it contributes nothing to the trace. Standard in trace-ideal theory: Simon, Trace Ideals §1-3; Reed-Simon, Functional Analysis I §VI.6; for the locally convex setting, Treves, Topological Vector Spaces §47-51. The manuscript collects it as Lemma C.3.2.2 in an appendix titled 'Preparations on locally convex topological vector spaces', and uses it at txt 25739 and txt 27805":
  forall o: Operator; forall w: HardySobolevSpace;
  factorsThroughASubspace(o, w) -> isNuclearOn(o, w) ->
  (isNuclearOn(theRestriction(o, w), theSubspace(o, w)) and
  trace(o, w) = trace(theRestriction(o, w), theSubspace(o, w)))

theorem theTraceIsComputedOnTheSubspace:
  forall o: Operator; forall w: HardySobolevSpace;
  factorsThroughASubspace(o, w) -> isNuclearOn(o, w) ->
  (isNuclearOn(theRestriction(o, w), theSubspace(o, w)) and
  trace(o, w) = trace(theRestriction(o, w), theSubspace(o, w)))
proof
  @conclusion |
    forall o: Operator; forall w: HardySobolevSpace;
      factorsThroughASubspace(o, w) -> isNuclearOn(o, w) ->
      (isNuclearOn(theRestriction(o, w), theSubspace(o, w)) and
      trace(o, w) = trace(theRestriction(o, w), theSubspace(o, w)))
    [by cite restrictingToAnInvariantSubspacePreservesTheTrace]
qed

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
// PERIODIZATION HAS A SECTION — Lemma 4.2.1.5's content.
//
// 12.4.1.1's proof needs a lift phi-tilde with sum_{xi in O*_{F,S}} phitilde(xi g) = phi(g),
// and the manuscript's justification is: "The existence of phitilde is ensured by discreteness
// of O*_{F,S} in GL_1(A_{F,S}) and compactness of the support of phitilde (see, for example,
// the proof of Lemma 4.2.1.5(2))" (txt 31211-31213).
//
// That is the classical fact that periodization over a discrete cocompact subgroup admits a
// section on compactly supported functions — build a smooth partition of unity subordinate to
// a fundamental domain. Not specific to this manuscript.

/// a function on the big group, and its periodization over a discrete subgroup
sort CompactlySupportedFunction
/// the subgroup acts discretely, and the function has compact support
pred isDiscreteWithCompactSupport(c: CompactlySupportedFunction)
/// a lift whose periodization is the given function
pred hasAPeriodizationSection(c: CompactlySupportedFunction)

intheory periodizationHasASection

hole periodizationAdmitsASection
  cites "standard: periodization over a discrete subgroup admits a section on compactly supported functions — given a discrete (indeed cocompact) subgroup, a smooth partition of unity subordinate to a fundamental domain produces a lift whose sum over the subgroup is the original function. Folland, A Course in Abstract Harmonic Analysis §2.6; Hewitt-Ross I §15; the construction is standard. The manuscript points at the proof of its own Lemma 4.2.1.5(2) for the same argument (txt 31211-31213), citing discreteness plus compactness of support":
  forall c: CompactlySupportedFunction;
  isDiscreteWithCompactSupport(c) -> hasAPeriodizationSection(c)

theorem periodizationHasASection:
  forall c: CompactlySupportedFunction;
  isDiscreteWithCompactSupport(c) -> hasAPeriodizationSection(c)
proof
  @conclusion |
    forall c: CompactlySupportedFunction;
      isDiscreteWithCompactSupport(c) -> hasAPeriodizationSection(c)
    [by cite periodizationAdmitsASection]
qed

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
// THE SCHWARTZ KERNEL THEOREM — Appendix C's Thm C.2.4.19.
//
// 12.4.1.1's computation turns an integral into an operator by the identification
// S(X, S(X)'_beta) ~= S(X) tensor-pi S(X)'_beta ~= L_beta(S(X)) (txt 31281-31284, "which is
// in turn linearly and topologically isomorphic, by Theorem C.2.4.19, to ... L_beta(S(A_F,S))").
//
// That is the Schwartz kernel theorem for nuclear spaces, which the manuscript collects in
// Appendix C ("Preparations on locally convex topological vector spaces"). It is the step
// that licenses computing with a kernel and reading off an operator, which is what the whole
// S-local calculation does.

/// a vector-valued Schwartz function, in the role of the computation's K
sort SchwartzKernelFunction
/// the continuous linear endomorphism it corresponds to
oper theCorrespondingEndomorphism(k: SchwartzKernelFunction) => Operator
/// the correspondence is a linear topological isomorphism at this kernel
pred correspondsToAnEndomorphism(k: SchwartzKernelFunction)

intheory schwartzKernelsAreEndomorphisms

hole theSchwartzKernelTheorem
  cites "standard: the Schwartz kernel theorem for nuclear spaces — S(X, S(X)'_beta) is linearly and topologically isomorphic to S(X) tensor-pi S(X)'_beta, hence to L_beta(S(X)), so every such vector-valued Schwartz function IS a continuous linear endomorphism. Treves, Topological Vector Spaces, Distributions and Kernels Thm 51.6 and §50; Grothendieck, Produits tensoriels topologiques Ch. II; Schwartz, Theorie des distributions. The manuscript collects it as Thm C.2.4.19 and applies it at txt 31281-31284":
  forall k: SchwartzKernelFunction; correspondsToAnEndomorphism(k)

theorem schwartzKernelsAreEndomorphisms:
  forall k: SchwartzKernelFunction; correspondsToAnEndomorphism(k)
proof
  @conclusion |
    forall k: SchwartzKernelFunction; correspondsToAnEndomorphism(k)
    [by cite theSchwartzKernelTheorem]
qed
